  {
    "title": "Case Study: Managing Single-Source Supplier Dependency Risk",
    "cat": "Advanced Practice",
    "body": [
      "Illustrative scenario: a specialised zipper has only one approved supplier. The supplier reports a longer lead time and a potential shortage during the peak season. Procurement cannot simply switch to a similar item because colour, dimensions, durability, appearance and test results may differ.",
      "Map the dependency: material code, styles using it, open POs, available stock, in-transit quantity, lead time, MOQ, supplier capacity, upstream raw-material sources and alternatives previously validated. Separate physical stock from quantities already allocated to other orders.",
      "Build scenarios for on-time delivery, partial delivery, late delivery and complete supply interruption. For each scenario, identify the date production would be affected, the number of styles at risk, allocation options, expedite cost and decisions requiring buyer approval. Do not treat a substitute as equivalent until technical evaluation is complete.",
      "Qualify a second source through technical documentation, samples, relevant testing, colour/dimension/function checks, garment-level pilot and formal change approval. If the material supports a protective claim or legal requirement, recheck the scope of the corresponding evidence and certification.",
      "Outputs should include a risk register, warning triggers, accountable owner, allocation options, second-source qualification plan and review date. Exercise: propose three measurable early-warning triggers rather than waiting until the line is already short of material.",
      "**Scope and input-data analysis.** Identify the material or process with a single source and measure dependency by purchase value, share of production, replacement lead time, technical exclusivity and impact of interruption. In apparel, exposure may involve specialised fabric, trims made with dedicated tooling, finishing processes, specialist printing or embroidery, or a subcontractor approved by the buyer. Distinguish “only one approved supplier” from “only one supplier exists in the market”; the mitigation options are different.",
      "**Operating design and control points.** Maintain a risk register covering the risk event, causes, evidence-based likelihood, order impact, detectability, recovery time and owner. Gather information on committed capacity, permitted financial-health indicators, upstream input dependencies, location, natural hazards, labour, compliance and business-continuity arrangements. Evaluate strategic stock, capacity reservation, a second source, tooling or recipe transfer, approved substitute materials, or redesign. For each option, include carrying cost, qualification time, testing, buyer approval and quality risk.",
      "**Reconciliation, exception handling and evidence.** Run a tabletop exercise in which the supplier stops deliveries for a defined period. Track affected orders, genuinely available stock, the projected stock-out date and decisions required. Prepare activation triggers, a contact group, approval authority for expediting or substitution, and buyer communications. Do not claim that an alternative source is ready before technical evaluation and required approvals are complete. After an exercise or actual disruption, update the risk score, evidence, actual recovery time and contract terms that may need renegotiation."
    ],
    "deep": "Single-source risk depends on technical substitutability, approval lead time, tooling, capacity and ownership of moulds or programmes. Identify materials and processes that cannot be transferred immediately. Build a dual-source plan with testing, volume allocation and sustainment criteria. Monitor financial, quality and lead-time signals from the sole source.\n\n**Scope and input-data analysis.** Identify the material or process with a single source and measure dependency by purchase value, share of production, replacement lead time, technical exclusivity and impact of interruption. In apparel, exposure may involve specialised fabric, trims made with dedicated tooling, finishing processes, specialist printing or embroidery, or a subcontractor approved by the buyer. Distinguish “only one approved supplier” from “only one supplier exists in the market”; the mitigation options are different.\n\n**Operating design and control points.** Maintain a risk register covering the risk event, causes, evidence-based likelihood, order impact, detectability, recovery time and owner. Gather information on committed capacity, permitted financial-health indicators, upstream input dependencies, location, natural hazards, labour, compliance and business-continuity arrangements. Evaluate strategic stock, capacity reservation, a second source, tooling or recipe transfer, approved substitute materials, or redesign. For each option, include carrying cost, qualification time, testing, buyer approval and quality risk.\n\n**Reconciliation, exception handling and evidence.** Run a tabletop exercise in which the supplier stops deliveries for a defined period. Track affected orders, genuinely available stock, the projected stock-out date and decisions required. Prepare activation triggers, a contact group, approval authority for expediting or substitution, and buyer communications. Do not claim that an alternative source is ready before technical evaluation and required approvals are complete. After an exercise or actual disruption, update the risk score, evidence, actual recovery time and contract terms that may need renegotiation.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Use an apparel-business scenario with a style code, PO, factory, materials and delivery milestones. For “Case Study: Managing Single-Source Supplier Dependency Risk”, build a data or process map identifying authoritative sources, revisions, owners, approval rights, exceptions and retained evidence. Create at least two failure scenarios (one data error and one operational failure); describe detection, containment, root cause, corrective action and effectiveness verification. Separate verified facts from assumptions. Do not invent legal limits or technical thresholds where no applicable control requirement has been specified.",
    "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase19-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Case Study: Managing Single-Source Supplier Dependency Risk\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Case Study: Managing Single-Source Supplier Dependency Risk</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#403b2c\">Case Study: Managing Single-Source Supplier Dependency Risk</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Supply flow and document control points</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#f2f1eb\" stroke=\"#88784e\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#403b2c\">Input data</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#f2f1eb\" stroke=\"#88784e\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#403b2c\">Analysis</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#f2f1eb\" stroke=\"#88784e\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#403b2c\">Reconciliation</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#f2f1eb\" stroke=\"#88784e\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#403b2c\">Decision</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Reconcile item codes, lots, revisions and transaction terms.</text></svg></figure>"
  },
  {
    "title": "Case Study: Reconciling Quantity, Quality and Payment After Delivery",
    "cat": "Advanced Practice",
    "body": [
      "Illustrative scenario: after delivery, the buyer reports missing cartons and claims that part of the product has a quality defect. Finance separately learns that the invoice is pending review. The teams need one shared shipment record and must distinguish quantity, quality, document and payment disputes.",
      "Compare the PO and approved amendments with the final packing list, count records, carton totals, transport documents, proof of delivery where available, inspection results and claim report. Establish whether the discrepancy arose during packing, handover, transport or receipt; do not assign responsibility from an aggregate total alone.",
      "For a quality claim, record defect code, inspected quantity, defective quantity, sampling method, photographs, lot, style, size/colour, detection conditions and notification date. Check the contract for notice periods, evidence requirements and remedies. Do not apply a generic claim percentage or compensation rule.",
      "Reconcile the invoice against price, accepted quantity, approved adjustments, chargebacks or credit notes where applicable, and payment terms. Separate disputed amounts from undisputed amounts. Payment or deduction must follow the contract and approval by authorised parties.",
      "Close the order with a report covering on-time delivery, quantity, quality, documents, additional costs, open receivables/payables, claim causes and lessons for the next PO. Exercise: design a reconciliation table with source, data owner, verification status and approver columns.",
      "**Scope and input-data analysis.** Start with one reconciliation row for each PO line or shipment line rather than immediately aggregating the order. Place side by side the ordered quantity, confirmed quantity, packed quantity, commercial-invoice quantity, transport-document quantity, quantity physically received and quantity accepted by the buyer after inspection. For quality, link each claim to style, colour, size, carton, material lot, inspection report and photographs. Distinguish shortage, over-delivery, incorrect size ratio, transit damage and rejection for specification nonconformity; each has a different remedy and evidence requirement.",
      "**Operating design and control points.** Build a reconciliation ledger with document source, revision, issue date, confirmer and dispute status. Reconcile money using the invoice, credit or debit notes, deductions, freight or insurance charges where allocated by contract, and bank payment records. Do not conclude that a deduction is valid merely because it appears on a remittance advice; check the contract, claim record and agreement between the parties. Separate exchange-rate differences and bank fees from quantity or quality variances so that they do not obscure the cause.",
      "**Reconciliation, exception handling and evidence.** Close the case with an agreed record of final quantity, claim status, amount payable or receivable, adjustment documents and approver. Retain proof of delivery, count records, QC results, claim correspondence and payment confirmation under the applicable retention policy. Identify preventive action at the point of failure: weighing or counting cartons, validating the packing list, locking invoice revisions or adding a pre-dispatch confirmation. State exactly where the error will be prevented and which data will provide an early warning next time."
    ],
    "deep": "Post-delivery reconciliation must connect shipped quantity, received quantity, variances, defective goods, claims, invoices and payments. Separate differences caused by packing, transport, counting and quality. Reconcile by PO, line and lot against the agreed tolerance terms. Close the order only when each variance has a documented disposition and complete records.\n\n**Scope and input-data analysis.** Start with one reconciliation row for each PO line or shipment line rather than immediately aggregating the order. Place side by side the ordered quantity, confirmed quantity, packed quantity, commercial-invoice quantity, transport-document quantity, quantity physically received and quantity accepted by the buyer after inspection. For quality, link each claim to style, colour, size, carton, material lot, inspection report and photographs. Distinguish shortage, over-delivery, incorrect size ratio, transit damage and rejection for specification nonconformity; each has a different remedy and evidence requirement.\n\n**Operating design and control points.** Build a reconciliation ledger with document source, revision, issue date, confirmer and dispute status. Reconcile money using the invoice, credit or debit notes, deductions, freight or insurance charges where allocated by contract, and bank payment records. Do not conclude that a deduction is valid merely because it appears on a remittance advice; check the contract, claim record and agreement between the parties. Separate exchange-rate differences and bank fees from quantity or quality variances so that they do not obscure the cause.\n\n**Reconciliation, exception handling and evidence.** Close the case with an agreed record of final quantity, claim status, amount payable or receivable, adjustment documents and approver. Retain proof of delivery, count records, QC results, claim correspondence and payment confirmation under the applicable retention policy. Identify preventive action at the point of failure: weighing or counting cartons, validating the packing list, locking invoice revisions or adding a pre-dispatch confirmation. State exactly where the error will be prevented and which data will provide an early warning next time.\n\n**Applied exercise:** Use an apparel-business scenario with a style code, PO, factory, materials and delivery milestones. For “Case Study: Reconciling Quantity, Quality and Payment After Delivery”, build a data or process map identifying authoritative sources, revisions, owners, approval rights, exceptions and retained evidence. Create at least two failure scenarios (one data error and one operational failure); describe detection, containment, root cause, corrective action and effectiveness verification. Separate verified facts from assumptions. Do not invent legal limits or technical thresholds where no applicable control requirement has been specified.",
    "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase19-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Case Study: Reconciling Quantity, Quality and Payment After Delivery\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Case Study: Reconciling Quantity, Quality and Payment After Delivery</title><text x=\"14\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#403b2c\">Case Study: Reconciling Quantity, Quality and Payment After Delivery</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Supply flow and document control points</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#f2f1eb\" stroke=\"#88784e\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#403b2c\">Input data</text><path d=\"M180 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#f2f1eb\" stroke=\"#88784e\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#403b2c\">Analysis</text><path d=\"M374 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"402\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#f2f1eb\" stroke=\"#88784e\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"484\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#403b2c\">Reconciliation</text><path d=\"M568 109 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"596\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"66\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#f2f1eb\" stroke=\"#88784e\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"678\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#403b2c\">Decision</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Reconcile item codes, lots, revisions and transaction terms.</text></svg></figure>"
  }
